13:42:39 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a totally fair reaction, and it's worth taking seriously rather than just saying "well, your teacher was wrong." A few things are true simultaneously here. **It wasn't fabricated maliciously — it came from real scientific observations, just connected wrong** The people who first pushed this idea weren't making things up from nothing. They noticed two true facts: 1. Glass is structurally amorphous, like a liquid, rather than crystalline like most solids. 2. Old windows are often thicker at the bottom. The error was inferring a causal link between them without actually calculating whether it was physically plausible. That's a reasoning failure, not a hoax. It then got repeated by people who found it a compelling, almost poetic idea — solids secretly still flowing, cathedrals as giant slow-motion experiments. It's a good story. Good stories spread faster than caveats. **Textbooks copy other textbooks** This is probably the biggest mechanism. Very few textbook writers go back to primary physics literature for every claim, especially for a fun aside like this one. They often lift explanations from previous textbooks or general science writing. If a claim gets into some influential mid-century text, it can get copy-pasted forward for generations. Nobody along the chain necessarily checked the viscosity math — it just felt physically reasonable given the "amorphous solid" fact, so it passed review after review. **Teachers largely trust their materials** Your teacher almost certainly didn't invent this — they taught what was in the curriculum or textbook they were handed. Teachers can't independently verify every fact in every subject; the system assumes vetting happened upstream. So this isn't really an indictment of your teacher's competence, it's a demonstration of how error can persist through an entire pipeline of otherwise careful people. **The physics community actually flagged this decades ago** This isn't new information — physicists and materials scientists have been pointing out the viscosity problem since at least the 1990s (there are calculations from people like Robert Brill at Corning going back even further, in the 1960s). Robert Zanotto's frequently cited 2007 paper made the case explicitly. Despite that, correcting embedded myths in K-12 science education is a slow, patchy process, because: - There's no central authority forcing every textbook publisher to update. - The myth isn't consequential enough (unlike, say, a medical error) to trigger urgent fixes. - It's charming and memorable, so some educators may keep it as a "fun fact" without rechecking it. **The broader lesson** This is a good case study in how scientific-sounding claims can persist in education for decades due to institutional inertia rather than any conspiracy — repetition substituting for verification. It's worth being appropriately skeptical of "fun facts" in general, even ones taught with confidence, and checking whether the actual physics holds up. In this case, the giveaway was that nobody who repeated the claim seems to have run the viscosity numbers before putting it in print.
