13:43:08 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: The pitch drop experiment is real—pitch does flow over decades—but glass operates in a completely different regime. Pitch's viscosity sits around 10^8 Pa·s at room temperature, which is enormous but still ten orders of magnitude lower than glass's 10^18-10^19 Pa·s. That's not just a matter of speed; it's a fundamental difference in how these materials behave, and a pitch drop takes roughly a decade to form. The irony is that the pitch experiment gets cited in popular science as evidence that amorphous solids can flow, which then reinforces the glass myth by association—even though pitch and glass have entirely different molecular structures and bonding. Pitch is viscoelastic, not amorphous in the same structural sense as silicate glass. If I scale pitch's timescale up to match glass's viscosity, the numbers become absurd—we're talking about timescales that dwarf the age of the universe. I want to use concrete numbers to show why "just even slower" fundamentally undersells the magnitude difference here, and walk through the calculation to make it clear why this comparison breaks down in any human-relevant timeframe.
13:43:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Good question, and the intuition makes sense on the surface—but the pitch drop experiment actually illustrates why the comparison breaks down, not why it holds. **What's actually happening in the pitch experiment** The University of Queensland experiment has run since 1927. Pitch (a derivative of tar) is sealed in a funnel, and drops fall roughly once a decade. It looks solid enough to shatter if hit with a hammer, yet it demonstrably flows. This part is completely real and not mythical at all. **The key difference: the numbers aren't just "a bit higher," they're astronomically higher** Pitch has a viscosity around 10^8 Pascal-seconds at room temperature. That's already enormous compared to something like honey (~10 Pa·s) or ketchup. But window glass sits around 10^18 to 10^19 Pascal-seconds — that's roughly 10 billion times more viscous than pitch. This isn't a difference of degree, it's a difference of regime. If pitch drips once a decade, scaling up to glass's viscosity doesn't give you "one drop per few centuries" — it gives you a timescale that dwarfs the age of the universe (13.8 billion years) many times over. Zanotto's calculations put meaningful glass flow at room temperature on the order of many billions of years to see any noticeable sagging, and that's likely a significant underestimate of the true timescale given how viscosity models behave at these extremes. **Why "just slower" is a misleading way to frame it** Our intuitions are bad at handling exponential differences. "Slower" suggests something like 2x, 10x, maybe 1000x — differences we can picture by analogy to everyday experience. But going from 10^8 to 10^18 isn't in that category at all. It's the difference between: - Something that visibly moves within a human lifetime (pitch), versus - Something where even if you had access to the entire history of the universe, you likely wouldn't see meaningful deformation (silicate glass at room temperature) **Why this comparison persists despite being flawed** The pitch drop experiment is often invoked in pop science specifically to make the glass claim sound more plausible—"see, this looks solid but flows, so why not glass too?" It's an intuitive rhetorical move, but it smuggles in an assumption that all high-viscosity amorphous materials are roughly interchangeable in behavior, just at different speeds. In reality, the actual physical property (viscosity) varies across these materials by many orders of magnitude, and that's not a minor detail — it's the entire ballgame in determining whether flow is observable in any humanly relevant timeframe. **A useful mental model** If pitch flowing over decades is like water eroding a rock over years, glass at room temperature is like asking whether that same rock will erode away if you stare at it for a single afternoon. Both processes are technically "the same kind of thing" in the loosest sense (viscous flow, erosion), but the timescales involved put them in entirely different practical categories. So the pitch drop experiment is a great demonstration that appearances (solid-looking) can be deceiving for viscosity — but it actually argues against, not for, extending that same logic to glass, once you look at the actual numbers involved.
