13:45:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're actually making a sharp and fair point, and I should be more careful with my language. "Zero" was too strong a claim on my part — I can't prove that, and no one has. **What's actually true, precisely stated** Nobody has proven glass has literally zero atomic motion or flow at room temperature. That's not how physics works — you generally can't prove an absolute zero for a continuous physical process. What has been done is: - Measuring/modeling the viscosity of silica glass at room temperature (~10^18–10^19 Pa·s) - Using that viscosity to calculate an expected flow rate under gravity for a given pane geometry - Finding that the predicted deformation over any realistic timeframe—decades, centuries, or even the age of the universe—is far smaller than atomic dimensions So the honest claim isn't "glass doesn't move at all," it's "the predicted motion, based on measured material properties, is so many orders of magnitude below any observable or even physically meaningful threshold that treating it as zero for practical purposes is justified." **Why "some tiny amount" doesn't rescue the original claim** Here's the key issue though: even granting you the point — let's say glass does creep some infinitesimal amount — this doesn't get you back to the cathedral window explanation, because the gap between "some flow exists in principle" and "enough flow to visibly thicken the bottom of a window pane over 700 years" is enormous. We're talking about needing millimeters of redistributed material. The calculated flow, even generously estimated, would produce something on the order of nanometers or less over that timeframe — utterly undetectable, let alone enough to reshape a windowpane. So you can be right that "not literally zero" is the more careful statement, while the original myth is still wrong, because the myth requires a flow rate that's many, many orders of magnitude larger than what any physical model of glass viscosity would predict. **A useful distinction: unmeasurable vs. nonexistent** This is similar to how physicists talk about proton decay. No one has observed a proton decay, and current theory says protons might not be perfectly stable — but the predicted half-life is so long (billions of times the age of the universe) that "protons are stable" is the practical, working statement, even though "literally zero decay, ever" isn't something anyone claims to have proven. Glass is the same kind of case. "Effectively solid, for all human and cosmological purposes we can test" is the accurate, defensible claim. "Absolutely zero movement, full stop, provably" overstates what physics can say. You're right to catch that distinction — I was sloppy stating it as zero rather than as immeasurably, functionally negligible. **Where that leaves us** So: you can hold onto "glass technically may have some nonzero flow rate" as a scientifically cautious position, and that's fair. What doesn't hold up is the specific historical claim that this flow is why cathedral windows are thicker at the bottom — that part is well-explained by manufacturing and installation practices, and the magnitude of any real flow is nowhere near sufficient to produce that effect on any human timescale.
