13:47:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That feeling makes sense — widespread belief usually is decent evidence for something, so it's reasonable to feel a pull toward "surely this many people can't be wrong." But it's worth looking at why that heuristic, which works most of the time, fails specifically for claims like this one. **Popularity tracks truth well for things people can check themselves** If "everyone" believes water boils at 100°C at sea level, that's solid evidence, because it's directly, repeatedly testable by tons of people in ordinary life. Widespread agreement is meaningful when the belief is exposed to real-world feedback that would correct it if false. **But it tracks something else entirely for claims people can't easily check** Nobody verifies glass viscosity in their own life. Nobody's watched a windowpane sag over 50 years and confirmed it against a control pane. The belief isn't sustained by people independently testing it and agreeing — it's sustained by the same story getting passed hand to hand: teacher to student, guidebook to tourist, documentary to viewer. In cases like this, "everyone believes it" mostly measures how good the story is at spreading, not how true it is. **Other examples of things "everyone knows" that are false** This isn't a weird one-off case. Lots of common knowledge fails the same way: - "We only use 10% of our brains" — false, thoroughly debunked, still said constantly - "Napoleon was unusually short" — false, he was roughly average height for his era; a propaganda/mistranslation artifact - "Bulls are enraged by the color red" — false, bulls are colorblind to red; they react to movement - "Goldfish have a 3-second memory" — false, studied and debunked repeatedly Every one of these persists today, confidently repeated by huge numbers of people, including many who are otherwise careful thinkers. What they share with the glass myth is that they're vivid, easy to picture, satisfying to repeat, and not something most people ever test directly. **The actual signal worth trusting here isn't popularity — it's checkability** The reason I'd bet on the viscosity calculation over popular consensus isn't "I distrust common knowledge on principle." It's that the viscosity numbers are independently measurable, published, and reproducible — anyone with the right equipment could verify them. "Everyone says so" isn't independently checkable in the same way; it's just a description of how far a story has spread, and good stories spread regardless of truth. **So it's not that weird, once you see the mechanism** It's not that a mysterious number of people are unusually gullible. It's that this particular kind of claim — vivid, hard to personally verify, tied to an authority (teacher, textbook, documentary) — is exactly the kind of claim that spreads on charm rather than accuracy. The fact that it's common knowledge tells you it's a good story. It doesn't tell you it's true.
