13:47:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get why it feels that way — after this many rounds of viscosity numbers, it probably does feel like I'm burying a simple point in complexity. But I'd push back gently on one thing: the viscosity math isn't overcomplicating the fact, it's actually the thing that tells you what the fact means. **Here's the core issue: "liquid" was never a purely structural word to begin with** When scientists (or anyone) call something a liquid, they're not just describing its atomic arrangement — they're making a claim about behavior: it flows, it takes the shape of its container, it doesn't hold a fixed form under its own structure. That's what distinguishes liquid from solid in ordinary usage and in physics. Structure (crystalline vs. disordered) is just one property a material has. Flow behavior is a separate property. In water, ice, and most materials, these two properties happen to travel together, so we never notice they're actually two different things. Glass is the case where they come apart: disordered structure, but solid behavior. So saying "glass is structurally a liquid" is true and interesting, but it's a claim about atomic arrangement, not a claim about whether it flows. Calling that "the only thing that matters" is essentially deciding that structure gets to define the word "liquid" all by itself — but that's not how the word has ever worked, for any other material. **Why the viscosity number isn't a side detail, it's the actual answer to the question you're asking** If your question is "does glass behave like a liquid," the viscosity is literally the measurement that answers that question. It's not extra math piled onto a simple fact — it's the specific data point that distinguishes "flows in observable time" from "doesn't." Skipping it doesn't make things simpler, it just means answering a behavioral question using only structural information, which doesn't actually settle it. **A short way to put the whole disagreement** You're treating "structurally a liquid" as sufficient grounds to call glass a liquid, full stop. I'm saying that's an incomplete standard, because "liquid" bundles two properties that happen to separate in this one material, and only one of them applies to glass at room temperature. That said — if you want to keep it simple for casual conversation and just say "well, structurally it's kind of like a liquid," that's a defensible, accurate thing to say. Where it stops being accurate is in the leap to "and therefore it visibly moves/oozes over centuries" — that specific behavioral claim is the part the physics doesn't support, regardless of how the structural classification shakes out.
