13:49:49 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get the appeal of that intuition, and it's not a dumb one — but it's worth noticing that "common sense about how the world works" is built entirely from our everyday human experience, which sits in a very narrow slice of reality. We evolved to intuit things like "heavy objects fall," "hot things burn," "sharp things cut" — stuff directly relevant to survival at human timescales and human senses. We did not evolve any intuition for what atoms do over 500 years, because no human has ever needed that intuition to survive. That's exactly where intuition tends to fail hardest — not in everyday physics, but in extreme scales: very large, very small, very slow, very fast, very hot, very cold. Common sense says the sun goes around the earth (looks that way), that heavier objects fall faster (they don't, air resistance aside), that a year is "a long time" (geologically it's nothing). None of these failures mean people are dumb — it means intuition is calibrated for a narrow range of experience, and confidently extends itself past that range anyway, because it doesn't come with a warning label saying "not valid outside normal human scales." "Glassy things feel like they'd ooze a little" is that same kind of extension — it feels right because glass looks unusual, vaguely liquid-ish, mysterious. But feeling physically plausible isn't the same as being physically true, and this is one of the clearest cases where they come apart, because we can actually measure the relevant property (viscosity) instead of just guessing from vibes. I'm not saying intuition is worthless — for most of daily life it's a great shortcut. I'm just saying this happens to be one of the specific spots where it breaks, and we know that because someone actually checked, rather than just going with what felt right.
