13:51:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's actually a sharper objection than it might seem at first, and I want to be careful not to overstate the counter-explanation just because it's the "correct" side of the myth. Let me actually tighten this up. **You're right that "every single installer consistently chose thick-down" would be a stretch if it required coordination** But it doesn't require coordination — it requires convergent, independent reasoning based on obvious physical logic, which is a very different (and much more plausible) claim. Compare it to something like: independent cultures across history all figured out that round wheels roll better than square ones, without any of them talking to each other. That's not a stretch because it doesn't require a conspiracy or agreement — it requires many people separately noticing the same physical fact (heavy side down = more stable) and acting on it. A glazier fitting an unevenly-thick pane into a lead came frame would likely notice pretty quickly, through simple handling, which orientation sat more securely, without needing to consciously theorize about it at all. **Where I should actually be more careful, and give you credit** I don't have good grounds to claim this was a universal, unbroken pattern across "every single installer across centuries." That's overstating the evidence. In reality: - Not all old windows are thicker at the bottom — there are documented cases of the reverse - Many old windows have been reinstalled, repaired, or replaced over centuries, so what you're looking at today isn't necessarily the original placement anyway - The tendency is a documented, common pattern, not an ironclad universal law So if I implied earlier that this was a clean, total explanation with no exceptions, that overstated the case. The honest version is: crown glass manufacturing genuinely produced uneven thickness (well documented, not in dispute), and thick-side-down was a common, sensible practical tendency — not a perfectly uniform rule enforced across every installation ever done. **Why gravity-pulled-glass still doesn't win by comparison, even with that concession** Even granting that the installation explanation isn't airtight in every instance, it still doesn't make "gravity slowly pulled it down" more plausible, because that explanation has to clear a much higher bar: it requires a measured material property (viscosity) to be wrong by many orders of magnitude. The installation explanation, even with some fuzziness around consistency, only requires "craftsmen tend to notice which way is more stable," which is a far lower bar to accept. So it's less "deliberate universal convention vs. simple gravity" and more "an imperfectly consistent but physically sensible practice vs. a physically implausible one." The installation explanation doesn't need to be perfect to still be far more plausible than the alternative.
