13:53:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a genuinely lovely way to think about it, and I don't think you have to fully give it up — I'd just gently push back on calling the real explanation "boring," because I think it's actually got its own kind of beauty, just a different one. **What you'd be trading, honestly** The flowing-window image gives you: time as a physical force acting on the building itself, matter quietly rearranging while nobody watches, the structure as something almost organic, alive in extreme slow motion. That's genuinely poetic — I'm not going to pretend it isn't. There's something appealing about a building that's still, in some small way, changing. **But here's what the real explanation actually gives you instead** A person, centuries ago, stood there holding an imperfect, hand-blown pane of glass — thick on one edge from being spun on a pontil rod over a fire, thin on the other — and made a small, practical judgment call: *this side goes down.* That decision, repeated by countless anonymous craftsmen across centuries and countries who never communicated with each other, converged on the same intuitive solution independently, purely because it's what made physical sense. What you're looking at in that window isn't the passage of time acting on inert material — it's a fossilized human decision. A tiny, preserved trace of someone's hands and judgment, seven hundred years ago, still sitting there in the glass exactly as they left it. That's not nothing. If anything, it's arguably more moving than the flow idea, because it's not really about the glass at all — it's evidence of a person. The window isn't alive; it's a message, unintentionally left behind, from someone who never imagined you'd be standing there wondering about it centuries later. **You don't have to fully let go of the other image either** Practically speaking, the "structurally amorphous, frozen mid-transformation" fact is still true and still a little eerie in its own right — glass genuinely is a snapshot of a liquid that got caught before it could organize itself into a crystal, like time got interrupted mid-motion. That part of the poetry is accurate. You can keep that. What doesn't hold up is the specific idea of it still moving now, today, while you're looking at it. But "frozen mid-transformation, forever, the moment it cooled" is its own kind of quietly beautiful, strange fact — arguably stranger than slow oozing, once you sit with it. It's not a lesser story. It's just a different one.
